A||N||10||peterh||Baaad Starter and the kindness of a stranger||||1||
Z||000000||peterh||06-12-2001||12:57 PM||||Last week I stopped at the King Kullen on the way home from work to load up with groceries. When I tried to start my 1986 SE V6 it would not start, not even a click although the gauges showed I had juice. As I was standing there waiting for my wife to come home to call her for a ride, a guy going to his car asked if I needed a lift. It turns out he lives very close to my house and was able to give me a ride home. About 4 hours later I drove over in my other car to the King Kullen parking lot and the Fiero started right up. I had replaced the starter 11 months ago for the same problem. Is there a high-torque started available for this engine? (And yes the car does does have the heat shield.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||skitime||06-12-2001||01:04 PM||||Did you replace the solenoid too?  Usually that problem is actually the solenoid and not the starter from my experiences.<p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||peterh||06-12-2001||01:07 PM||||I replaced the starter including the solenoid which is attached.<BR>The store I got the rebuild from is out of business so I can't complain to them, I think it had a 12month warranty.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||01:30 PM||||is there any chance you have an exhaust leak pointing hot gas at the starter. The fact that is started up again after it cooled down might be an indication of something.<P>  sometimes if a starter wont kick in at all, a good wack on the side with a wrench or hammer will wake it up.<P>  mine has been acting flakey. Sometime when I turn the key it kicks in but doesnt spin. Then if i try it again it starts right up.<P>  I suspect mine is on its last leg.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||01:34 PM||||I had a bug that did the same thing as your fiero once. Wanted to go out for lunch, turned the key several times, nothing.<P>  after work I gave it another try and it started right up, and I never had any more trouble with the starter on that car.<P>  I can see how people can start believe in supernatural stuff when unexplainable things like this happens - there sure is no logical explaination for it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||06-12-2001||01:46 PM||||Check your cables too.  I had an old big block Trans Am that would heat soak the starter even with the heat shield.  It didn't damage the starter, but when hot it required just enough more voltage that the old wiring couldn't supply the juice.  New heavy duty cables fixed that right up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||onfire||06-12-2001||02:06 PM||||Also have the battery checked. My friends Fiero did the same thing this past Sunday. Luckily we were at Shuck's Auto Supply. After 20 minutes of messing with the starter, we took the battery in to have it tested. The battery had a warranty so he got it replaced for free. Nothing like breaking down at an Auto Parts store.||||reg||1
Z||000007||deceler8||06-12-2001||02:26 PM||||If it happens again, try starting it with the tilt wheel in the full down position.<P>Might be something flaky in your steering column...I had this problem and it starts fine with the wheel in the down position.||||reg||1
Z||000008||starfighter007@msn.com||06-12-2001||02:54 PM||||you have a starter problem  and yes it is probaly the solenoid.either replace with a new/rebuild starter assy.from ohhhh I dont know maybe auto zone with lifetime warr. or or replace just the solenoid either way easy enough to do and cheap enough but get it done before you get stranded ||||reg||1
Z||000009||starfighter007@msn.com||06-12-2001||02:59 PM||||oh I forgot to add something very important make sure you install the heat shield that covers the solenoid if it dont have one on it now get one at auto zone while you are getting your starter assy or solenoid   ||||reg||1
Z||000010||starfighter007@msn.com||06-12-2001||03:02 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by deceler8:<BR><B>If it happens again, try starting it with the tilt wheel in the full down position.<P>Might be something flaky in your steering column...I had this problem and it starts fine with the wheel in the down position.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>sounds like your ignition/starter switch is out of adjustment<BR><p>[This message has been edited by starfighter007@msn.com (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
